The Core Efficiency Principle: 8-Way Symmetry
The foundation of efficient circle generation is 8-way symmetry. A circle centered at the origin
(0,0) is symmetric across the x-axis, y-axis, and the diagonal lines y = x and y = -x.
Consequently, the algorithm only needs to compute pixel coordinates for one octant (from 90° to
45°, where x = 0 to x = y). The other seven reflection points are mapped simultaneously using
simple sign changes and coordinate swaps:
● (x, y)
● (y, x)
● (-y, x)
● (-x, y)
● (-x, -y)
● (-y, -x)
● (y, -x)
● (x, -y)

If the circle is centered at Xc, Yc instead of the origin, you simply shift every plotted pixel by
adding the center offsets: Xc+x, Yc+y

